        "content": "Cotton, known as the 'White Gold', is India's preeminent commercial fiber crop, sustaining millions of farmers and driving the textile manufacturing industry.\n\n### Botanical Classification of Cultivated Cotton\n- **Old World / Asiatic Diploids ($2n = 26$):**\n  1. *Gossypium arboreum:* Indigenous to India/Asia; coarse, short staple (12\u201324 mm), low spinning count, high resistance to drought, sucking pests, and fungal diseases.\n  2. *Gossypium herbaceum:* Native to Africa/Arabian Peninsula; medium staple (15\u201323 mm), non-shattering closed bolls, suited to harsh rainfed tracts.\n- **New World Allotetraploids ($2n = 52$):**\n  3. *Gossypium hirsutum* (American upland cotton): Accounts for >90% of Indian acreage; medium to long staple (20\u201332 mm), high yield potential, wide adaptability.\n  4. *Gossypium barbadense* (Egyptian / Sea Island cotton): Extra-long staple (>35 mm), silky fiber, high spinning quality (>80s counts), demanding high-management irrigated environments.\n\n### Bt Cotton Technology and Resistance Management\nGenetically modified Bt cotton expresses crystalline endotoxin proteins (*Cry1Ac*, *Cry2Ab*) derived from the soil bacterium *Bacillus thuringiensis*. The ingested protoxin is solubilized in the alkaline gut (pH >9.0) of lepidopteran larvae, cleaved into active delta-endotoxin, and binds to cadherin receptors on midgut epithelial cells, creating lytic pores that cause gut paralysis and death. To delay insect resistance in *Helicoverpa armigera* and *Pectinophora gossypiella* (Pink bollworm), non-Bt cotton 'refugia' (structured 20% refuge or 5% refuge-in-a-bag) must be maintained to sustain homozygous susceptible alleles.\n\n### Square Dropping and Nutrient Management\n'Square' is the young triangular floral bud. Shedding of squares and young bolls (frequently 60\u201370%) occurs due to ethylene synthesis triggered by cloudy weather, soil moisture swings, or hormonal imbalance. Foliar application of Planofix (alpha-naphthyl acetic acid - NAA @ 10\u201320 ppm) at square initiation and peak flowering arrests abscission. Balanced nutrition requires split nitrogen applications, foliar spraying of 2% potassium nitrate ($KNO_3$), and magnesium sulfate ($MgSO_4$ @ 1%) to prevent 'reddening of leaves' (necrotic chlorosis caused by Mg deficiency).",
