        "id": "sec-agp218-4-2",
        "heading": "2. Sugarcane (Saccharum spp.): Planting, Ratooning, and Ripening",
        "content": "Sugarcane is a long-duration perennial C4 cash crop cultivated for sucrose production, biofuel ethanol, and co-generation of biomass power.\n\n### Species Complex of Saccharum\n- **Wild Species:** *Saccharum spontaneum* (Kans; hardy, rhizomatous, high fiber, disease-resistant) and *Saccharum robustum* (tall, thick stalk, low sucrose).\n- **Cultivated Species:** *Saccharum officinarum* ('Noble Cane'; thick juicy stalks, high sucrose, low fiber, but susceptible to diseases and frost; $2n = 80$), *Saccharum barberi* (North Indian cane; thin stalk, frost-hardy), and *Saccharum sinense* (Chinese cane).\n- **Nobilization of Sugarcane:** Pioneer Indian cane breeder Sir T.S. Venkatraman at ICAR-Sugarcane Breeding Institute (SBI), Coimbatore, crossed *S. officinarum* with *S. spontaneum* and *S. barberi*, transferring disease resistance and hardiness into sweet noble backgrounds to develop landmark interspecific commercial hybrids like **Co 205** and **Co 0238**.\n\n### Modern Planting Techniques and Sett Preparation\nSugarcane is vegetatively propagated using stem cuttings termed **setts**. Top one-third to one-half portion of the stalk is ideal for setts as it contains higher moisture, glucose, and nitrogen, ensuring rapid bud sprouting.\n- **Sett Treatment:** Dipping 2- or 3-budded setts in Carbendazim 0.1% + Chlorpyriphos solution at 50\u00b0C for 20 minutes (or hot-water treatment at 52\u00b0C for 30 minutes) controls seed-borne red rot (*Colletotrichum falcatum*) and grassy shoot phytoplasma.\n- **Ring-Pit Method:** Digging circular pits (90 cm diameter, 45 cm depth) spaced 120 cm apart allows concentric sett placement, yielding >150\u2013180 t/ha with 30\u201340% water savings.\n- **Single Bud Settling / Sustainable Sugarcane Initiative (SSI):** Raising nursery seedlings in pro-trays and transplanting 30-day-old settlings at 1.5 m row spacing reduces seed cane requirement from 8 t/ha to just 1 t/ha.\n\n### Ratoon Management and Trash Mulching\nRatooning (harvesting the main plant crop and allowing stubble buds to sprout into succeeding crops) saves land preparation and seed costs. Ratoon management involves: (1) Stubble shaving flush with soil surface; (2) Shoulder harrowing to prune old roots; (3) Gap filling; (4) Band placement of 25% extra nitrogen; and (5) Spreading dry cane trash (5\u20137 t/ha) as **trash mulch** in alternate rows, which suppresses weeds, conserves soil water, and recycles potash upon decomposition.\n\n### Ripening Indices and Chemical Ripeners\nHarvest maturity is determined when hand refractometer Brix reaches 18\u201320% (juice sucrose >16%) and the top-to-bottom Brix ratio approaches 0.95\u20131.0. Foliar spray of chemical ripeners like Glyphosate (@ 600\u2013800 g a.i./ha as Polaris) or Ethrel (ethephon @ 1000 ppm) 6\u20138 weeks before harvest accelerates sucrose accumulation.",
