        "content": "Sunflower is a premier non-traditional oilseed crop characterized by exceptional day neutrality (photo-insensitivity), enabling its cultivation across Kharif, Rabi, and Zaid seasons.\n\n### Floral Biology and Seed Setting Constraints\nThe sunflower inflorescence is a **capitulum** (head) consisting of two flower types: sterile, colorful peripheral **ray florets** (attracting pollinators) and hundreds of fertile, hermaphroditic, tubular **disc florets** arranged in logarithmic spirals. Disc florets exhibit **protandry** (anthers mature and shed pollen 1\u20132 days before stigmas become receptive), rendering the crop virtually 100% self-incompatible and entomophilous.\n- **Seed Setting Failure:** Peripheral florets set plump seeds, whereas central disc florets frequently produce chaffy, empty achenes. Causes include: (1) Inefficient natural honeybee pollination; (2) Vascular bottleneck in nutrient translocation to the center of the large head; (3) Boron deficiency.\n- **Remedial Interventions:**\n  - Hand pollination (rubbing capitula with muslin-cloth-covered palms during 8:00\u201311:00 AM) at alternating days during flowering, or maintaining 4\u20135 bee hives/ha.\n  - Foliar application of **Borax (0.2%)** at ray floret opening stage to facilitate pollen tube growth and sugar translocation, decreasing hollow seeds by 30\u201340%.\n\n### Oil Chemistry and Fatty Acid Profile\nSunflower seed contains 40\u201344% high-quality edible oil. The oil is rich in heart-healthy polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA), predominantly **linoleic acid (55\u201365%)** and **oleic acid (25\u201335%)**, with very low saturated fatty acids (<10%). Sunflower oil contains high concentrations of alpha-tocopherol (Vitamin E), conferring natural oxidative stability.",
