        "content": "Sesame (Til), often crowned the 'Queen of Oilseeds', is one of the oldest oilseeds domesticated in India. It possesses 48\u201352% oil and 20\u201325% protein, highly prized for culinary and industrial uses.\n\n### Agronomic Hardiness and Shattering Behavior\nSesame is an exceptionally drought-hardy crop adapted to light, well-drained soils, thriving under high temperatures ($27\\text{\u2013}35^\\circ\\text{C}$). However, it is highly sensitive to waterlogging and soil salinity.\n- **Capsule Dehiscence / Shattering:** Traditional sesame capsules split open along longitudinal carpellary sutures upon physiological maturity, dropping seeds onto the ground. This indeterminate ripening causes 15\u201330% harvest losses. Harvesting must be performed when bottom capsules turn yellowish-green, followed by curing upright in stooks ('shocks') on threshing floors.\n\n### Sesame Phyllody: Etiology and Vector Control\nPhyllody is the most catastrophic disease of sesame in India, causing up to 80\u201390% yield collapse.\n- **Etiology:** Caused by 'Candidatus Phytoplasma asteris', a wall-less, pleomorphic mollicute residing in the sieve phloem elements. It suppresses floral homeotic genes.\n- **Symptoms:** Floral parts transform into green, stunted, leafy, sterile structures (virescence and phyllody). Internodes shorten drastically, causing extreme bushy stunting ('witches broom'). The plant fails to form capsules.\n- **Transmission & Control:** Transmitted by the leafhopper (jassid) **Orosius albicinctus**. Managed by spraying systemic insecticides like Dimethoate 30% EC (1.5 ml/L) or Imidacloprid (0.3 ml/L) at early stages, removing volunteer weed reservoirs, and planting barrier rows of pearl millet.\n\n### Biochemical Stability: Sesamol and Sesamolin\nSesame oil exhibits legendary oxidative shelf-life without becoming rancid. This resistance to rancidity is imparted by unique endogenous lignan antioxidants: **sesamin** and **sesamolin**. Upon roasting or bleaching, sesamolin hydrolyzes into **sesamol**, an extraordinarily potent phenolic free-radical scavenger that protects unsaturated fatty acids from auto-oxidation.",
