      "modelAnswer": "Cultivated cotton (*Gossypium* spp.) comprises four distinct species belonging to two ploidy groups:\n\n1. **Old World / Asiatic Diploids ($2n = 26$):**\n   - *Gossypium arboreum:* Indigenous to India and the Indo-Burma region. Known as 'Desi cotton', it produces short, coarse fibers (staple length 12\u201324 mm) suitable for low spinning counts (up to 20s). Highly resistant to drought, sucking pests, and fungal wilts.\n   - *Gossypium herbaceum:* Native to tropical Africa and the Arabian peninsula. Produces medium-coarse fibers (15\u201323 mm) in non-shattering closed bolls, adapted to arid rainfed conditions.\n2. **New World Allotetraploids ($2n = 52$):**\n   - *Gossypium hirsutum:* Native to Central America/Mexico (Upland cotton). Accounts for >90% of global and Indian production. Produces medium-to-long staple (20\u201332 mm) fibers with high yield potential, adaptable to diverse agro-climatic conditions.\n   - *Gossypium barbadense:* Native to South America (Egyptian/Sea Island cotton). Produces extra-long staple (>35 mm), silky, high-tenacity fibers that spin into premium high counts (>80s). Requires intensive management and long growing seasons.",
