      "modelAnswer": "Prior to the 1920s, North Indian sugarcane cultivation relied on indigenous subtropical species (*Saccharum barberi* and *S. sinense*), which were thin-stalked, highly fibrous, low in sucrose (10\u201312%), and low yielding, but hardy and frost-tolerant. Conversely, tropical 'noble cane' (*Saccharum officinarum*), grown in South India, possessed thick juicy stalks and high sucrose (16\u201318%), but failed catastrophically in North India due to severe winter cold, drought, and susceptibility to red rot.\n\n### The Nobilization Process\nPioneered by Sir T.S. Venkatraman and C.A. Barber at the Sugarcane Breeding Institute (SBI), Coimbatore, 'nobilization' involved systematic interspecific hybridization. They crossed *S. officinarum* ('Vellai') with the wild, rhizomatous species *S. spontaneum* (Kans) to introduce disease resistance and vigor, and subsequently backcrossed the hybrids with *S. barberi* to impart subtropical adaptability and cold tolerance.\n\n### Impact on Indian Sugar Industry\nThis breeding breakthrough produced landmark commercial hybrids such as **Co 205**, **Co 213**, and **Co 419**, expanding sugarcane cultivation across the Indo-Gangetic plains. Cane productivity tripled, juice purity surged, and India achieved self-sufficiency in crystal sugar manufacturing.",
