      "syllabusTopic": "Sugarcane (Saccharum spp.): planting geometry, sett treatment, ratoon management, trash mulching, and ripening",
      "questionText": "Describe the key management practices required for sustaining high productivity in ratoon sugarcane.",
      "modelAnswer": "Ratooning is an integral component of sugarcane production, accounting for over 50% of total cane acreage in India. A successfully managed ratoon saves seed cane purchase and land preparation expenses while maturing 3\u20134 weeks earlier than plant cane. Sustaining yields >80\u2013100 t/ha in ratoons requires five crucial agronomic operations:\n\n1. **Stubble Shaving:** Harvest the plant crop flush with the ground using sharp cane harvesting knives. If stalks are cut high, above-ground stubble buds produce weak, aerial tillers that desiccate easily. Shaving stimulates deep subterranean buds to sprout vigorous, well-anchored tillers.\n2. **Trash Disposal and Mulching:** Spread dry cane trash (5\u20137 t/ha) as mulch in alternate rows. Trash mulching suppresses weed flushes, minimizes soil evaporative water loss during hot summer months, and recycles 30\u201340 kg $K_2O$/ha upon decomposition.\n3. **Shoulder Harrowing / Off-barring:** Break the compacted ridges using tractor cultivators to prune old, decaying root systems and loosen the soil, stimulating fresh adventitious root flushes.\n4. **Gap Filling:** Fill gaps exceeding 40\u201350 cm within 30 days of harvest using pre-germinated single-bud polybag settlings to maintain an optimal plant stand.\n5. **Nutrient Top-Dressing:** Ratoons require 20\u201325% more nitrogen than plant crops due to lower early mineralization; apply full $P$ and $K$ along with one-third $N$ immediately after stubble shaving.",
