      "questionText": "Explain the biochemical and microbiological process of water retting in jute and what causes the 'Shyamla' defect.",
      "modelAnswer": "Water retting is an eco-biochemical process wherein the non-cellulosic cementing matrix\u2014primarily pectins, hemicelluloses, and gums\u2014binding bast fiber bundles to the inner woody stem (stick or hurd) is decomposed by microbial enzymes, freeing commercial fiber strands.\n\n### Microbiological Progression\nHarvested jute bundles are submerged in slow-moving, clean water ($32\\text{--}34^\\circ\\text{C}$). Initially, aerobic bacteria utilize soluble sugars in the bark, depleting dissolved oxygen. Anaerobic, spore-forming, pectinolytic bacteria (predominantly *Clostridium butyricum*, *Bacillus macerans*, and *B. pumilus*) rapidly proliferate. These bacteria secrete extracellular pectinases\u2014specifically **endo-pectin lyase (PNL)** and **polygalacturonase (PG)**\u2014that hydrolyze calcium and magnesium pectate in the middle lamella. Retting is complete in 12\u201318 days when fibers separate freely from the woody core upon manual rubbing.\n\n### Etiology of the 'Shyamla' Color Defect\nWhen farmers weigh down submerged bundles with riverbed clay/mud, banana pseudo-stems, or rusted iron bars, soluble ferrous/ferric ions ($Fe^{2+}/Fe^{3+}$) and polyphenolic tannins dissolve into the retting water. The iron reacts chemically with plant tannins, forming an insoluble, deep grey-to-black iron-tannate precipitate that permanently stains the fibers. This dark, dull discoloration is termed **'Shyamla' (blackish) fiber**, severely depressing market value and industrial dyeability.",
