      "questionText": "Why does sunflower experience poor seed filling in the center of the head, and how can it be overcome?",
      "modelAnswer": "Sunflower (*Helianthus annuus* L.) heads frequently suffer from 'hollow seediness' or central seed sterility, where outer peripheral florets develop plump, oil-rich achenes while central florets produce empty, chaffy hulls. This defect reduces commercial seed yield by 20\u201340%.\n\n### Physiological and Biological Causes\n1. **Protandry and Self-Incompatibility:** Sunflower disc florets exhibit protandry (pollen sheds before stigmas become receptive). The plant is highly self-incompatible and entomophilous. Peripheral florets open first and receive abundant pollinator visits; as flowering proceeds toward the center, pollinator visits decline.\n2. **Centripetal Vascular Bottleneck:** Vascular traces radiate from the stem outward to the head periphery before branching inward. Developing outer seeds act as immediate proximal sinks, monopolizing carbohydrates and water. The central disc florets suffer acute assimilate starvation.\n3. **Boron Deficiency:** Boron is critical for pollen tube elongation and sucrose translocation; deficient soils restrict central fertilization.\n\n### Remedial Management Practices\n- **Supplementary Hand Pollination:** Gently rub the capitula during 08:00\u201311:00 AM using a muslin-cloth mitten on alternating days during flowering.\n- **Bee Hives:** Install 4\u20135 colonies of honeybees (*Apis cerana* / *Apis mellifera*) per hectare.\n- **Foliar Boron Nutrition:** Spray **0.2% Borax** (2 g/L) or 0.1% solubor at the ray floret opening stage to ensure pollen germination and assimilate transport to central florets.",
