      "questionText": "What causes sesame phyllody, how is it transmitted, and what are its symptoms and management?",
      "modelAnswer": "Sesame phyllody is the most catastrophic disease affecting *Sesamum indicum* in India, frequently inflicting 80\u2013100% seed yield loss under early infection.\n\n### Causal Organism and Vector Transmission\nThe disease is caused by **'Candidatus Phytoplasma asteris'**, a wall-less, pleomorphic, phloem-limited mollicute. It is transmitted in a persistent, circulative-propagative manner by the cicadellid leafhopper (jassid) **Orosius albicinctus**. The phytoplasma multiplies inside the salivary glands of the vector and is introduced into host phloem sieve elements during feeding.\n\n### Characteristic Symptoms\n- **Phyllody (Floral Virescence):** The hallmark symptom is the complete homeotic transformation of floral organs into abnormal green, leafy structures. Calyx, corolla, stamens, and carpels become leafy, sterile appendages.\n- **Witches' Broom:** Extreme shortening of internodes accompanied by rampant proliferation of stunted axillary buds gives the upper plant canopy a dense, bunched 'witches' broom' appearance.\n- **Total Pod Sterility:** Infected flowers fail to set capsules; any formed capsules remain small, cracked, and completely seedless.\n\n### Integrated Disease Management\n1. **Vector Control:** Spray systemic insecticides like Dimethoate 30% EC @ 1.5 ml/L or Imidacloprid 17.8% SL @ 0.3 ml/L at 20 and 35 DAS to suppress jassid populations.\n2. **Cultural Sanitation:** Rogue and burn infected plants immediately upon visual detection to eliminate inoculum sources; keep borders free of reservoir weed hosts (*Parthenium*, *Acanthospermum*).\n3. **Host Resistance:** Cultivate tolerant varieties such as RT 125, RT 127, and TKG 22.",
