      "questionText": "Explain the biochemical basis of the exceptional oxidative stability of sesame oil.",
      "modelAnswer": "Sesame oil is widely recognized among vegetable fats for its extraordinary shelf-life and stability against auto-oxidative rancidity, even when exposed to high ambient temperatures or prolonged open storage. This stability is driven by unique endogenous lignan antioxidants:\n\n1. **Natural Lignans (Sesamin and Sesamolin):** Crude sesame oil contains substantial concentrations (0.5\u20131.2%) of the lipid-soluble furofuran lignans **sesamin** and **sesamolin**.\n2. **Thermal Hydrolysis to Sesamol:** During seed roasting or commercial oil refining, sesamolin undergoes thermal and acid-catalyzed hydrolysis, cleaving into **sesamol** (3,4-methylenedioxyphenol) and sesamindiol. Sesamol possesses a free phenolic hydroxyl group that acts as an exceptionally powerful hydrogen donor, scavenging lipid peroxyl radicals ($LOO^\\bullet$) and terminating free-radical chain reactions.\n3. **Synergy with Tocopherols:** Sesame oil naturally contains gamma-tocopherol (Vitamin E). The combination of sesamol and gamma-tocopherol displays potent biochemical synergism, neutralizing reactive oxygen species (ROS) far more effectively than either compound in isolation.\n4. **Food Processing Significance:** Because sesamol does not degrade at frying temperatures, sesame oil preserves stability during deep frying and protects blended culinary oils from rancidity.",
