      "questionText": "Formulate a comprehensive production technology guide for Bt cotton cultivation in Central India, detailing planting geometry, nutrient management, square shed prevention, and sucking pest management.",
      "modelAnswer": "Bt cotton is the cornerstone of commercial agriculture in Central India (Maharashtra, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh). Maximizing productivity (>2.5 t/ha seed cotton) requires systematic integration of canopy architecture, fertility, and pest management.\n\n### 1. Soil Selection and Seedbed Preparation\nBt cotton thrives on deep black cotton soils (Vertisols) with high water retention capacity and pH 7.0\u20138.2. Deep chisel plowing once every three years breaks subsoil hardpans, followed by two cross-harrowings to create a clod-free seedbed. Planting on ridges and furrows (spaced 90\u2013120 cm apart) or the Broad Bed and Furrow (BBF) configuration is vital to prevent waterlogging during monsoons and allow furrow irrigation during dry spells.\n\n### 2. Planting Geometry and Refugia Compliance\n- **Hybrids & Spacing:** Sow BG-II hybrids (e.g., RCH 659, MRC 7351) at 90 cm \u00d7 60 cm in rainfed Vertisols, or 120 cm \u00d7 60 cm under drip-irrigated conditions (plant population: 13,800 to 18,500 plants/ha).\n- **Non-Bt Refugia:** Sow 5 border rows of non-Bt isogenic cotton around the Bt plot (or blend 5% non-Bt seed in 'Refuge-in-a-Bag' lots) to preserve susceptible pink bollworm alleles.\n\n### 3. Balanced Nutrient Management\nRecommended fertilizer dose for rainfed Bt hybrids is 120:60:60 kg N:P2O5:K2O/ha (elevated to 150:75:75 kg/ha under irrigation):\n- **Basal:** Apply 100% P and 50% K alongside 20% N at sowing.\n- **Splits:** Top-dress remaining N and K in 3 equal splits: at square initiation (35\u201340 DAS), peak flowering (60\u201365 DAS), and early boll formation (80\u201385 DAS).\n- **Foliar Sprays:** Spray 2% Potassium Nitrate ($KNO_3$) or 1% 19:19:19 at 60 and 80 DAS to feed expanding boll sinks. To prevent leaf reddening, spray 1% Magnesium Sulfate ($MgSO_4$) twice during peak boll development.\n\n### 4. Preventing Square and Boll Dropping\nExcessive square shedding occurs due to hormonal imbalances triggered by cloudy weather or moisture swings. Spray Planofix (alpha-NAA @ 10\u201320 ppm or 1 ml per 4.5 L water) at square initiation and peak flowering to arrest abscission.\n\n### 5. Integrated Management of Sucking Pests\nBt endotoxins control only lepidopteran caterpillars; sucking pests must be managed proactively:\n- **Seed Treatment:** Imidacloprid 600 FS @ 5 ml/kg seed shields seedlings for 30 days.\n- **Cultural & Biological Control:** Install yellow sticky traps (15/ha) for whiteflies and blue sticky traps for thrips; release *Chrysoperla carnea* @ 10,000 predators/ha.\n- **Chemical Interventions:** If jassids or whiteflies exceed Economic Threshold Levels (ETL: 2 jassids/leaf or 6\u20138 whiteflies/leaf), spray Flonicamid 50% WG @ 150 g/ha, Diafenthiuron 50% WP @ 600 g/ha, or Spiromesifen 22.9% SC @ 500 ml/ha.",
