      "questionText": "Compare the two cultivated jute species (Corchorus olitorius vs C. capsularis) and provide a detailed analysis of the BIS jute grading parameters and defects.",
      "modelAnswer": "Jute (*Corchorus* spp.) is India's principal commercial bast fiber. The industry is founded on two cultivated species that diverge in botany, agronomic adaptation, and commercial utility.\n\n### 1. Botanical and Agronomic Comparison\n\n| Agronomic Parameter | Tossa Jute (*Corchorus olitorius*) | White Jute (*Corchorus capsularis*) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Common Name** | Mitha pat (Sweet jute) | Tita pat (Bitter jute, contains corchorin) |\n| **Origin** | Africa / India | Indo-Burma region |\n| **Soil & Topography** | Well-drained uplands; intolerant of waterlogging | Lowlands and floodplains; highly flood-tolerant |\n| **Pod Morphology** | Long, cylindrical, 5-valved pods | Small, rounded, globular, wrinkled capsules |\n| **Fiber Characteristics** | Golden-yellow/brown, silky, high tensile strength | Off-white/cream, coarser, lower tensile strength |\n| **Spinning Counts** | High spinning counts (>20s to 30s) | Low spinning counts (10s to 16s; sacking/bags) |\n| **Popular Cultivars** | JRO 524 (Navin), JRO 204, JRO 8432 | JRC 321, JRC 212, JRC 517 |\n\n### 2. Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) Raw Jute Grading System\nBIS categorizes raw jute into 8 commercial grades (Grade 1 being finest, Grade 8 being poorest) based on six weighted physical parameters:\n1. **Strength (Tenacity):** The most critical parameter, measured in g/tex using fiber bundle strength testers. Grade 1 requires tenacity >28 g/tex, whereas Grade 8 exhibits <15 g/tex.\n2. **Fineness:** Expressed as linear density (tex). Finer fibers spin into higher yarn counts without breaking.\n3. **Color and Luster:** Tossa fibers range from bright golden yellow to creamy silver. Stained, dull fibers are downgraded.\n4. **Root Content:** Percentage of hard, woody, unretted bark remaining at the basal cut ends (cut-root). Top grades must have <5% root content; lower grades tolerate >30%.\n5. **Length and Freedom from Defects:** Fiber bundles should be >2.5 m long, clean, and free from tangled runners.\n\n### 3. Commercial Retting Defects\n- **Shyamla Color:** Blue-black/dark grey discoloration caused by mud/banana weighing weights forming iron-tannate precipitates.\n- **Barky / Knotty Fiber:** Incomplete retting of the basal stem leaving hard, unseparated gummy bark patches.\n- **Dazed Fiber:** Weak, rotten, lusterless fibers caused by over-retting, where bacteria attack the cellulose walls.\n- **Specky Fiber:** Tiny black spots adhering to fibers due to mite punctures or fungal lesions.",
