      "syllabusTopic": "Sesame (Sesamum indicum): agronomic management, capsule shattering, phyllody phytoplasma, and antioxidant stability",
      "questionText": "Formulate a complete package of practices for rainfed Kharif sesame cultivation, addressing soil management, varietal selection, capsule shattering mitigation, and post-harvest handling.",
      "modelAnswer": "Sesame (*Sesamum indicum* L.) is an ancient, drought-tolerant kharif oilseed suited to semi-arid rainfed agro-ecosystems. Achieving yields >750\u20131,000 kg/ha requires careful agronomic management.\n\n### 1. Soil and Land Preparation\nSesame thrives in well-drained, sandy loam to light clay loam soils with pH 6.0\u20137.5. It cannot tolerate standing water or saline-alkaline soils. Prepare a fine, weed-free seedbed through one deep plowing followed by two harrowing passes and firm planking. Field leveling is critical; stagnant water for >24 hours causes devastating seedling root asphyxiation and mortality.\n\n### 2. Sowing Time, Seed Rate, and Spacing\n- **Sowing Window:** Sow with the onset of monsoon (late June to early July). Early sowing avoids heavy jassid flights and phyllody disease.\n- **Seed Rate & Depth:** Sowing rate is 4\u20135 kg/ha. Because sesame seeds are tiny (1,000-seed weight is 2.5\u20133.5 g), mix seed with dry fine sand or sieved FYM (1:4 ratio) to achieve uniform distribution at a shallow sowing depth of 2.0\u20132.5 cm.\n- **Spacing & Thinning:** Maintain 30 cm between rows and thin seedlings to 10\u201315 cm plant-to-plant distance at 15\u201320 DAS (target population: ~220,000 plants/ha).\n\n### 3. Varietal Selection\nSelect certified high-yielding cultivars adapted to local zones:\n- **White-Seeded (Export Quality):** RT 351, TKG 22, RT 127 (high oil, bolder seeds).\n- **Brown/Black-Seeded:** TMV 3, TMV 7, Pragati.\n- **Phyllody-Tolerant Cultivars:** RT 125, Gujarat Til 2, Swetha.\n\n### 4. Nutrient and Weed Management\n- **Fertilizer Dose:** Apply 30:15:15 kg N:P2O5:K2O/ha. Apply half N and all P and K basally; top-dress the remaining N at flowering (30 DAS). Apply elemental sulfur or gypsum (200 kg/ha) to enhance oil synthesis.\n- **Weed Control:** Critical period of weed competition is the first 15\u201330 days due to slow early canopy growth. Apply Pendimethalin 30% EC @ 0.75 kg a.i./ha as pre-emergence within 48 hours of sowing, complemented by one hand weeding at 20 DAS.\n\n### 5. Harvest Management and Shattering Mitigation\nSesame exhibits indeterminate ripening; lower capsules mature while upper stems continue flowering. To prevent seed shattering losses:\n- **Harvest Timing:** Harvest when leaves turn yellow and drop, and bottom capsules turn yellowish-green (before capsules split open).\n- **Curing in Shocks:** Cut plants manually near base, tie into small bundles, and stand them upright in conical heaps ('shocks' or stooks) on a clean concrete floor or tarpaulin for 7\u201310 days. Capsules complete ripening safely in the shock.\n- **Threshing:** Invert bundles and shake gently; dry seeds to <7% moisture before storage in sealed bins.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Edaphic requirements: sandy loams and zero waterlogging tolerance",
        "Seeding specifications: 4-5 kg/ha mixed with sand, 30x10 cm spacing, shallow planting",
        "Cultivars for export white seeds (RT 351) and phyllody tolerance (RT 125)",
        "Nutrient management (30:15:15 NPK + sulfur) and Pendimethalin weed control",
        "Harvesting protocol at yellowish-green capsule stage and conical shock curing"
      ]
