      "questionText": "Compare the agronomic and technological attributes of Desi cotton (Gossypium arboreum) versus American upland cotton (Gossypium hirsutum).",
      "modelAnswer": "Desi cotton (*Gossypium arboreum*) and American upland cotton (*Gossypium hirsutum*) represent contrasting evolutionary lineages adapted to distinct agro-ecological niches:\n\n1. **Ploidy and Genetics:** *G. arboreum* is an Old World diploid ($2n = 26$, A-genome) indigenous to the Indian subcontinent. *G. hirsutum* is a New World allotetraploid ($2n = 52$, AADD-genome) originating from Central America.\n2. **Fiber Quality and Spinning Count:** *G. hirsutum* produces superior medium-to-long staple lint (24\u201332 mm) with high fiber fineness and tenacity, spinning into yarn counts of 30s to 50s. *G. arboreum* produces short (14\u201322 mm), coarse lint with thick cell walls, suited only for low counts (<20s) or non-textile industrial uses (surgical cotton, mattresses, absorbent pads).\n3. **Biotic and Abiotic Hardiness:** *G. arboreum* possesses exceptional genetic resistance to sucking pests (jassids, whiteflies), Cotton Leaf Curl Virus (CLCuV), fungal root rots, and severe drought. *G. hirsutum* is highly susceptible to CLCuV and sucking pests, requiring intensive pesticide and fertilizer inputs.\n4. **Input Responsiveness:** Under irrigated, high-fertility management, *G. hirsutum* yields 2.5\u20133.5 t/ha. *G. arboreum* has lower yield potential under high inputs, but delivers stable, reliable yields in harsh, rainfed marginal tracts.\n5. **Commercial Share:** *G. hirsutum* accounts for >90% of Indian cotton acreage, whereas *G. arboreum* is restricted to specialized rainfed pockets.",
