      "questionText": "Differentiate between plant cane and ratoon cane across growth duration, root development, nutrient requirements, and commercial yield.",
      "modelAnswer": "Sugarcane cultivation involves alternating cycles of 'plant cane' (established from stalk cuttings/setts) and 'ratoon cane' (regenerated from subterranean stubble buds):\n\n1. **Establishment and Sprouting:** Plant cane is raised by placing fresh vegetative setts into the soil, requiring 30\u201345 days to achieve full sprouting. Ratoon cane originates from established rootstocks; stubble buds sprout rapidly within 10\u201315 days after harvest.\n2. **Growth Duration and Maturity:** Plant cane requires 11 to 14 months to complete vegetative and ripening ontogeny. Ratoon cane matures 3 to 4 weeks earlier (10 to 12 months) due to an established root system and accelerated early canopy development.\n3. **Root System Architecture:** Plant cane initiates temporary 'sett roots' followed by permanent 'shoot roots'. Ratoon cane relies initially on old, decaying parent roots before fresh root flushes emerge from active subterranean nodes, requiring shoulder harrowing to prune old rootstocks.\n4. **Nutrient Requirements:** Ratoon cane requires 20\u201325% more nitrogen than plant cane because early root absorption is sluggish and soil microbial immobilisation of carbonaceous trash temporarily ties up available mineral nitrogen.\n5. **Economic Yield and Production Cost:** Plant cane incurs high production expenses (seed setts, land preparation, planting labor). Ratoon cane cuts production costs by 25\u201330%, but yields 10\u201320% less millable cane than the parent plant crop unless gap filling and fertilization are aggressively maintained.",
