      "syllabusTopic": "Jute (Corchorus spp.): species differences, retting biochemistry, ribbon retting, and fiber quality grading",
      "questionText": "Compare conventional whole-plant water retting with modern mechanical ribbon retting in jute.",
      "modelAnswer": "Retting is the critical technological operation determining the grade and value of jute bast fiber. Conventional water retting and ribbon retting contrast sharply in operations and quality:\n\n1. **Material Handled:** Conventional retting submerses entire harvested whole plants (stems with bulky woody cores, weighing 30\u201340 t/ha). Ribbon retting strips the green fiber-containing bark (ribbon) using a mechanical decorticator immediately after harvest, discarding the inner woody stick in the field and handling only 8\u201310 t/ha of ribbons.\n2. **Water Requirements:** Conventional retting requires massive volumes of slow-moving water (water-to-stalk ratio of 1:20), needing large community ponds. Ribbon retting reduces water requirements by 65\u201370%, allowing retting in small poly-lined micro-pits (1:5 water-to-ribbon ratio).\n3. **Retting Duration:** Conventional whole-plant retting requires 14\u201320 days depending on ambient water temperature. Ribbon retting, especially when inoculated with microbial cultures (*CRIJAF Sona*), completes in just 6\u20138 days.\n4. **Pollution and Odor:** Conventional retting rots massive woody core tissue, generating severe foul odors, anaerobic hydrogen sulfide emission, and high biological oxygen demand (BOD). Ribbon retting causes negligible aquatic pollution and no foul odor.\n5. **Fiber Quality and Defects:** Conventional retting frequently suffers from uneven retting along the stalk and 'Shyamla' discoloration from contact with bottom mud. Ribbon retting produces uniformly retted, bright golden-lustrous fiber free of mud stains, upgrading the fiber by 1\u20132 commercial grades.",
